## <a name="BKMK_NUnit"></a>NUnit Tests for `ArrayList`
In NUnit, you would define a test fixture and use attributes to identify the test methods. Here's an example of how you can test some capabilities of the `ArrayList` class.

```csharp
using NUnit.Framework;
using System;
using System.Collections;

[TestFixture]
public class TestArrayList
{
    private ArrayList arrList;

    [SetUp]
    public void Init()
    {
        arrList = new ArrayList();
        arrList.Add(1);
        arrList.Add(2);
        arrList.Add(3);
    }

    [Test]
    public void Count_AfterAddingElements_ShouldReturnCorrectCount()
    {
        Assert.AreEqual(3, arrList.Count);
    }

    [Test]
    public void Remove_ElementInArrayList_ShouldReturnTrue()
    {
        Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Remove(1));
    }

    [Test]
    public void Remove_NonExistentElement_ShouldReturnFalse()
    {
        Assert.IsFalse(arrList.Remove(10));
    }

}
```

Please note that the above tests are very basic and assume that the `ArrayList` class is functional. For more thorough testing, you should consider what your `ArrayList` class may be doing in a more complicated scenario, such as error handling, or corner cases. Additionally, it's probably best to test the `ArrayList` class in isolation so you aren't indirectly testing other parts of your system that rely on `ArrayList`.

Also keep in mind the fact that `ArrayList` is not generally recommended for new development due to performance issues; newer collection types in .NET like `List<T>` or `HashSet`, which are more performant and offer more functionality, should be considered for new development.  

Remember, the main aim of a testing is to verify that the system under test works as per the specifications. The above examples are very basic and may not cover all possible test scenarios. A complete test suite would need to cover several possible behaviors and edge conditions.

**NOTE**: The tests in the above examples are only examples and are meant to give a simple understanding only. They are not sufficient for robust test coverage. 

## <a name="BKMK_XUnit"></a>XUnit tests for `ArrayList` 
It's quite similar to using another testing tool like `NUnit` to test the code. XUnit, however, does not use attributes to tag test methods and fixture setup, instead it uses convention-based approach where test classes are expected to be found and invoked automatically.

Here's an example of how you can test some capabilities of the `ArrayList` class using XUnit:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections;
using Xunit;

public class TestArrayList
{
    private ArrayList arrList;

    public TestArrayList()
    {
        arrList = new ArrayList();
        arrList.Add(1);
        arrList.Add(2);
        arrList.Add(3);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void Count_AfterAddingElements_ShouldReturnCorrectCount()
    {
        Assert.Equal(3, arrList.Count);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void Remove_ElementInArrayList_ShouldReturnTrue()
    {
        Assert.True(arrList.Remove(1));
    }

    [Fact]
    public void Remove_NonExistentElement_ShouldReturnFalse()
    {
        Assert.False(arrList.Remove(10));
    }
}
```

The above XUnit tests are in a format that’s more practical for testing classes without complex setups or teardown methods. However, since the example does not encompass any complex scenarios or edge cases, the tests in XUnit alone won't cover all possible test scenarios.

