A: You can use Assert.AreNotSame or Assert.AreEqual methods to verify the results after removing the element. Here is an example:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void TestMethod1()
{
    //arrange
    ArrayList lst = new ArrayList() {1,2,3,4};
    int countBefore = lst.Count;

    //act
    lst.Remove(2);
    int countAfter = lst.Count;

    //assert
    Assert.AreNotEqual(countBefore, countAfter);
}
```

In this example, I add an element to an ArrayList, record the count of the list before the remove operation, perform the remove operation, then check the count after the operation. The Assert.AreNotEqual method will throw an exception if the two values are the same, which means the remove operation did not reduce the count of the ArrayList. 

You may want to make other assertions depending on your requirements. For example, you can check if the item was removed from the ArrayList by looking for the item in the ArrayList after the remove operation and verifying that its absence in the ArrayList results in the not existence of an element in the ArrayList that can be found with lst.Contains(item).  

To do this, your assertion could look like this:

```csharp
Assert.IsFalse(lst.Contains(2));
```

This assertion checks that the item was effectively removed from the ArrayList. The item is searched for in the ArrayList after the remove operation, and since it should not be there, the method Contains() should return False, which throws an exception if it does not. 

I hope this helps, 

Cheers,
Aravinth_Karnati

PS - As per your request, your assertions should be placed in Public Test methods, not within private methods in class. And the Public Test methods should be accessible outside the class. So you have to put your assertions outside of your method - i.e., outside the private methods and inside a public method. 

If you have further questions or need more help, please feel free to reach out.

